<p>
  A naive alternative is to generate directly in pixel space. However, when we strip the VAE out of a unified model and try to diffuse raw pixels, the generation quality collapses. The model must learn both high-level semantic layout and fine-grained visual details from the same raw signal.
</p>
<p>
  Representation Forcing solves this by utilizing the visual representations from the model's own jointly trained <strong>understanding encoder</strong> as an intermediate structural scaffold.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────── §2 Online Quantization -->
<section id="s-online-vq" data-code="online_vq">
<div class="section-no">02 — Online Quantization</div>
<h2>Online vector quantization without pretrained tokenizers</h2>
<p>
  To let the decoder predict high-level structures, the continuous representations from the understanding encoder are discretized online into a sequence of representation tokens. Discretization naturally factorizes representation from rendering, prompting the predicted tokens to retain structural features while discarding low-level pixel noise.
</p>
<p>
  Since the encoder is jointly trained, its features change online. We use an <strong>Exponential Moving Average (EMA)</strong> copy of the image encoder to extract slow-moving, stable target features.
</p>

