  As described in Algorithm 1, we perform cosine similarity mapping between patch features and the learnable prototypes. A <strong>Sinkhorn-Knopp</strong> clustering balance iteration is applied online to ensure uniform prototype usage and prevent codebook collapse.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────── §3 Token Sequence & Mask -->
<section id="s-sequence" data-code="sequence">
<div class="section-no">03 — Token Sequence & Mask</div>
<h2>Putting representations in context</h2>
<p>
  How do predicted representation tokens guide pixel generation? They are placed directly in the sequence. The model operates on a unified sequence:
</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Sequence Layout</div>
  <span class="math-var">Sequence</span> = [ <span class="math-var">Text Tokens</span> , <span class="math-var">Representation Tokens</span> , <span class="math-var">Pixel Patches</span> ]
</div>
<p>
  During self-attention inside the transformer backbone, a custom causal-to-bidirectional mask controls the attention flow, acting as the structural conditioning channel:
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <div class="vp-title">attention_flow_explorer.html</div>
    <div class="vp-hint">Hover over tokens to see attention mask routing</div>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="seq-box" id="seq-box">
      <!-- Text -->
      <div class="seq-token token-text" data-idx="0">"a"</div>
      <div class="seq-token token-text" data-idx="1">"red"</div>
      <div class="seq-token token-text" data-idx="2">"apple"</div>
      <div class="seq-token token-text" data-idx="3">"on"</div>
      <div class="seq-token token-text" data-idx="4">"a"</div>
      <div class="seq-token token-text" data-idx="5">"blue"</div>
      <div class="seq-token token-text" data-idx="6">"plate"</div>
      <!-- Rep -->
      <div class="seq-token token-rep" data-idx="7">Rep₀</div>
      <div class="seq-token token-rep" data-idx="8">Rep₁</div>
      <div class="seq-token token-rep" data-idx="9">Rep₂</div>
      <div class="seq-token token-rep" data-idx="10">Rep₃</div>
      <!-- Pixels -->
      <div class="seq-token token-pixel" data-idx="11">Pix₀</div>
      <div class="seq-token token-pixel" data-idx="12">Pix₁</div>
      <div class="seq-token token-pixel" data-idx="13">Pix₂</div>
      <div class="seq-token token-pixel" data-idx="14">Pix₃</div>
    </div>
    
    <div id="mask-status" style="font-size:0.75rem; color:var(--muted2); font-family:monospace; line-height:1.4;">
      Hover over a token to visualize the attention pattern.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Hovering over different token positions illustrates the core design:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Text Tokens</strong> attend causally to preceding text.</li>
  <li><strong>Representation Tokens</strong> attend causally to preceding text and representations.</li>
  <li><strong>Pixel Patches</strong> attend bidirectionally to all other pixel patches, and causally to all preceding text and representation tokens, pulling structural layouts into pixel generation.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────── §4 Guided Pixel Generation -->
<section id="s-pixel-gen" data-code="flow_matching">
<div class="section-no">04 — Guided Pixel Generation</div>
<h2>Generating pixels via Flow Matching</h2>
<p>
  Once the representation tokens are predicted autoregressively at inference, they serve as the structural framework. The model then performs iterative denoising starting from Gaussian noise z₀ ~ N(0, I) using flow matching.
</p>
<p>
  Let's compare the denoising trajectory between naive pixel-space generation and Representation Forcing.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <div class="vp-title">denoising_flow_simulation.canvas</div>
    <div class="vp-hint">Denoising comparison: Naive vs RF</div>
  </div>
